# ── Go build outputs ──
bin/
dist/
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.test
*.out
coverage.html
coverage/
vendor/

# Go profiling
*.prof
*.pprof

# ── Go build cache — a project-local GOCACHE ──
# GOCACHE is normally per-user (~/Library/Caches/go-build), but an agent or CI
# job working in parallel worktrees points it at $WORKTREE/.gocache so
# concurrent builds don't contend on one shared cache. That makes tens of
# thousands of files appear inside the project, which `git add -A` would
# otherwise sweep into a commit.
#
# `forge lint` does NOT depend on this rule: its generated-file scan skips any
# directory carrying Go's own GOCACHE sentinel README, whatever it is named
# (see internal/commitpolicy.IsGoBuildCacheDir). A cache entry is a verbatim
# copy of compiler input, generated Go source included, so without that skip
# every entry looked like generated code that had been wrongly ignored.
.gocache/

# Go workspace — local, per-developer dev-loop wiring; NEVER committed.
# The root go.mod already carries `replace <module>/gen => ./gen`, so a
# plain `go build ./...` resolves root↔gen with no go.work at all. A
# committed go.work is therefore both redundant AND a pollution hazard:
# the moment someone adds a sibling `use ../forge` (to build against a
# local, unpublished forge), that absolute/relative path breaks every
# other clone and CI. Keep it local. `forge new` scaffolds a starter
# go.work (`use ( . gen )`) for workspace mode on this machine — and
# `forge project new --link-forge` also adds a `use <local-forge>` so the
# project builds against your in-development forge checkout. Either way it
# stays ignored. go.work.sum is a generated lock file.
go.work
go.work.sum

# ── Dev web-runtime bridge — the npm twin of go.work above ──
# A DEV build of forge writes an npm workspace ROOT at the project root
# (package.json + .forge-link/<symlink>) that points this project's frontends
# at a local @reliantlabs/forge-web-runtime checkout, so edits there are live
# here with nothing published. A released forge never writes it.
#
# Each frontend's OWN package.json is tracked and keeps the published semver
# range — the bridge redirects that requirement by workspace name, so no
# machine-specific path is ever committed. These entries are anchored with a
# leading "/" on purpose: a bare `package.json` would also ignore
# frontends/*/package.json, which MUST stay tracked.
/package.json
/package-lock.json
/node_modules/
/.forge-link/

# ── Node.js / Next.js ──
node_modules/
.next/
.turbo/
.vercel/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ── Generated code is COMMITTED ──
#
# gen/ (buf's Go stubs, gen/go.mod, forge_descriptor.json), every `*_gen.go`,
# the forge-generated mocks, and each frontend's src/gen/ are checked in
# deliberately. Nothing here ignores them, and `forge lint` fails if a rule
# anywhere in the project does.
#
# They used to be ignored on the reasoning that `forge generate` re-creates
# them, so committing them was redundant. What that cost was far worse than
# the duplication: a fresh clone or `git worktree add` did not compile until
# someone ran codegen, so CI, every new contributor and every throwaway
# worktree started from a broken tree — a scaffolded project's CI was red on
# `undefined: UnauthenticatedProcedures` because pkg/middleware/*_gen.go was
# ignored and no CI job regenerates. It also made generator upgrades
# invisible: a forge bump that changes emitted code produced no diff to
# review, and `forge ci verify-generated` (which diffs with `git status`)
# cannot see an ignored file at all.
#
# The rule is that a checkout builds as-cloned, and regenerating is a no-op:
# if `forge generate` produces a diff, commit it in the same change that
# caused it.

# ── Machine-local forge state (NEVER committed) ──
#
# .forge-kcl/ is where older forge versions copied their KCL module into the
# project. forge now supplies `import forge` from the binary that is
# rendering (docs: forge's ADR 0003), so nothing writes this directory any
# more and `forge generate` deletes a leftover one. It stays ignored because
# a committed copy pins one forge build's schemas into every clone and CI
# checkout; `forge lint` fails if one is tracked.
.forge-kcl/

# Seed data materializes at RUNTIME (`forge db seed` / `forge env up`
# auto-seed) — forge writes no seed files into the project. db/seeds/custom/
# is your hand-authored demo-data overlay and IS committed. Legacy
# auto-generated db/seeds/*.sql / db/fixtures/ from older forge versions can
# be deleted.

# ── IDE ──
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ── Secrets ──
# secrets/<env>.yaml is the FileSecrets store deploy/kcl/<env>/main.k
# declares: a flat YAML map of env-var name to value. forge resolves
# `sensitive` config fields (DATABASE_URL, …) from it for host runs and for
# the local-cluster Secret it renders. It must never reach git.
#
# Set values with `forge secret set <env> <KEY>` (or hand-edit the YAML). A
# value only reaches a service that DECLARES it via EnvVar.secret_ref, so a
# key nothing references is inert; non-secret config belongs in
# deploy/kcl/<env>/config.k where it stays version-controlled.
secrets/

# ── Environment ──
# forge projects do NOT use .env files: their contents bypassed KCL (the
# whole file was injected into every host service), which is what pulled
# config out of version control. Ignored so a stray one can never be
# committed; `forge lint` fails on any that appear.
.env
.env.*

# ── OS ──
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# ── Logs ──
*.log

# ── Local data / state ──
# Never commit on-disk databases, Docker volumes, or temp dirs.
data/
tmp/
*.air.toml.bak

# ── Forge internal state ──
# .forge/ contains debug-session.json, caches, .forge/state/build-<env>.json
# (handoff from `forge env build --push` to `forge env deploy`), transient side
# renders (.forge/render/ holds `--explain-drift` diff inputs), and
# other runtime state. Generated files certify THEMSELVES (each carries
# an embedded `forge:hash` marker), so there is no generated-state
# manifest to commit. Three exceptions are negated back in and MUST be
# committed when they exist:
#   disowned.json   — one-way ownership transfers (`forge disown`):
#                     path + reason + timestamp. Travels with the repo
#                     so forge never regenerates a disowned file in any
#                     clone or worktree.
#   hashes.json     — scoped render hashes for the few generated files
#                     whose format cannot carry an embedded marker
#                     (JSON outputs only).
#   scaffolded.json — the scaffold-once BIRTH LEDGER: every "yours" file
#                     forge has ever scaffolded here. It is what makes
#                     DELETING a scaffold stick — without it, an absent
#                     file is indistinguishable from one never written
#                     and the next `forge generate` hands it back. Must
#                     travel with the repo: a teammate's fresh clone has
#                     to inherit "we removed that file", not re-acquire
#                     it on their first generate. To want one back:
#                     `forge project rescaffold <path>`.
#   releases/       — the release ledger (`forge env build --release`,
#   promotions/       `forge env deploy <env> <version>`): what each version
#                     IS and which version each environment runs, as an
#                     append-only log
#                     per env. Only for envs that keep their ledger in the
#                     repo; an env declaring forge.ControlPlane keeps it on
#                     the control plane instead.
# Everything else under .forge/ is per-developer state and intentionally
# excluded by the broad children rule below.
#
# NOTE: this must be `.forge/*` (children), not `.forge/` (the dir):
# git cannot re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is excluded, so a
# `.forge/` + `!.forge/<file>` pair would silently ignore the file and
# it would never get committed. (Found the hard way in cp-forge — see
# its .gitignore fork note.)
.forge/*
!.forge/disowned.json
!.forge/hashes.json
!.forge/scaffolded.json
!.forge/releases/
!.forge/releases/*.json
!.forge/promotions/
!.forge/promotions/*.jsonl

# ── What NOT to ignore ──
#
# The following are scaffolded once by `forge new` and then owned by you.
# `forge upgrade` uses checksum protection (not .gitignore) to detect and
# preserve your edits. Commit them.
#
#   cmd/*.go            — entrypoints (package main)
#   pkg/middleware/*.go  — non-generated middleware (scaffolded, user-owned)
#   CHANGELOG.md         — project changelog
#   docs/                — ADRs, runbook, SLO definitions
#
# The following encode shared project conventions for LLM-driven
# development and are INTENTIONALLY committed:
#
#   .reliant/                           — forge-managed conventions, project metadata, skills
#   reliant.md / CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md  — user-owned project memory file (depends on --memory flag)
#   .cursorrules                        — project memory file (cursor format)
#   .github/copilot-instructions.md     — project memory file (copilot format)
#   .mcp.json                           — project-level MCP server config
#   .mcp.json.example                   — documentation of opt-in MCP servers
#
# Do not add these to .gitignore. If you need to keep local-only overrides,
# create a `.mcp.local.json` or similar and ignore that instead.
